<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    "Gold": the dark Exhale mark — the gold "E))" lit against near-black — kept selectable from
    Settings → Appearance → App icon.

    Sources are assets/icon_dark.png (the finished dark tile) and assets/splash_dark.png (the glyph
    alone on transparency), split the same three ways as @mipmap/ic_launcher: full-bleed
    background, glyph-only foreground, monochrome for themed icons. See that file for why the
    finished tile is never handed to an adaptive icon whole.

    The background is not a scaled copy of the tile. It is the glyph's own halo, generated by
    blurring the foreground layer exactly where the foreground layer sits — because the launcher
    scales the two layers independently as it parallaxes and masks them, and a halo baked at a
    different scale from its glyph slides off it and reads as a smear. All three layers are
    generated per density from the two sources above; regenerate them as a set, never by hand.

    The glyph is placed at the gold set's span (199/432 of the layer) so the two icons are the same
    optical size sitting next to each other in the drawer.
-->
<adaptive-icon xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">
    <background android:drawable="@mipmap/ic_launcher_gold_background" />
    <foreground android:drawable="@mipmap/ic_launcher_gold_foreground" />
    <!--
        The monochrome layer is shared with @mipmap/ic_launcher on purpose, not by omission.

        A themed icon is a single-colour stencil filled by the launcher's palette, and both packs
        are the same "E))" glyph — so the two would render identically anyway, and keeping a second
        copy would only be a second thing to forget to update.
    -->
    <monochrome android:drawable="@mipmap/ic_launcher_monochrome" />
</adaptive-icon>
